  "summary": "A traditional Swedish farmstead characterized by buildings painted with the distinctive Falu red pigment derived from copper mine waste.",
  "description": "Features wooden structures painted with a vibrant reddish-brown color contrasting with white trim; developed using copper mine byproducts as a wood preservative. The color became a national symbol and was widely adopted due to its protective qualities and aesthetic appeal.",
  "culturalContext": "Falu red — copper-mine sludge pigment cooked into linseed paint — turned Sweden's farmhouses, barns and manors a single crimson from the 1700s, when Falun's Stora Kopparberg mine supplied a national economy of timber buildings needing preservation. 19th-century National Romantic writers made the red cottage an emblem of Swedish identity, and heritage practice now guards the hue on farmsteads across the country.",
